+        // Every record must be a multiple of 8 bytes, and the copy below
+        // stores it into the buffer as 8-byte words. Do not replace that copy
+        // with std::memcpy, and do not add a byte-wise fallback for other
+        // sizes.
+        //
+        // Without USE_ASYNC_STREAM the buffer is mapped pinned host memory,
+        // so every store in the copy becomes a PCIe write to host memory.
+        // Device code gives std::memcpy no store width: for records like
+        // CUTracer's it compiles to a loop of one-byte loads and stores
+        // (LD.E.U8 + ST.E.U8), one PCIe write per record byte. On B200 hosts
+        // that write stream took GPUs off the bus (Xid 79) within seconds to
+        // minutes whenever the buffer sat on the NUMA node remote to the GPU:
+        // about once a second the GPU's traffic to host memory stalled for 10
+        // to 110 ms, and the PCIe link went down at the start of one of those
+        // stalls. The same records stored as 8-byte words ran without stalls.
+        // A size that is not a multiple of 8 is a caller bug, so it traps here
+        // rather than fall back to the byte copy.
+        //
+        // Buffer offsets stay 8-byte aligned: every reservation is a multiple
+        // of 8, and the buffer comes from cudaHostAlloc or cudaMalloc.
+        if (nbytes % 8 != 0) {
+            __trap();
+        }
